the target language is: lps.

% Conway's Game of Life: an intensional fluent recomputed over a grid every
% cycle. The stress case.
% Source: legacy_lps1/examples/CLOUT_workshop/life.pl
%
% The grid, the neighbour offsets and the list recursion that counts living
% neighbours are Prolog, and live in the companion file life.lps
% (docs/le_lps_surface.md §7). What stays in English is the part that is about
% Life rather than about lists: when a cell dies, and when one comes alive.

the maximum time is 8.

the actions are:
    *a cell* dies; known as die.
    *a cell* comes alive; known as live.

the fluents are:
    *a cell* lives; known as alive.
    *a cell* has *a number* living neighbours; known as aliveNeighbors.
    *a number* of *a list* live; known as countLivingNeighbors.

the templates are:
    *a cell* is on the grid; known as cell.
    the neighbours of *a cell* are *a list*; known as adjacent.

the knowledge base life includes:

initially 2-3 lives
    and 3-3 lives
    and 4-3 lives.

% The one genuinely temporal definition: how many of a cell's neighbours are
% alive right now. The counting itself is countLivingNeighbors/2 in life.lps.

a cell has a number living neighbours at a time if
    the neighbours of the cell are a list
    and a number of the list live at the time.

if a cell lives at a first time
    and the cell has a number living neighbours at the first time
    and the number < 2
then the cell dies from the first time to a second time.

if a cell lives at a first time
    and the cell has a number living neighbours at the first time
    and the number > 3
then the cell dies from the first time to a second time.

if a cell is on the grid
    and the cell has 3 living neighbours at a first time
then the cell comes alive from the first time to a second time.

when a cell comes alive
then the cell lives.

when a cell dies
then it is not the case that the cell lives.
